.TH PICOTOP 1 "November 15, 2026"
.SH NAME
picotop \- display processor, memory, storage and process activity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B picotop
[
.B \-np
] [
.B \-d
.I seconds
] [
.B \-c
.I count
] [
.B \-s
.I order
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Picotop
redraws one screen of activity every few seconds: a bar of the processor
time spent in user, niced and system state, a history graph of the busy
share, level bars for the memory in core, swap, each mounted file system
and the battery, the temperature and supply voltage, the transfer rate of
each drive, the event rates and queue lengths, the state of the second
core, and the process table.
.PP
A share is the change of a tick count between two refreshes divided by the
change of the total; the first screen has no earlier reading and shows the
shares since boot.
A process marked
.B +
is in core.
A reading the running kernel does not serve prints
.BR "not in this kernel" .
.PP
The kernel records no command name for process 0, the swapper, so its row
shows
.BR swapper ,
the name ps(1) gives it.
.PP
One process is in core at a time on this system, so each refresh that
finds another process running makes the kernel exchange the two with swap.
A longer interval lowers that cost.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.BI \-d " seconds"
Seconds between refreshes, 1 to 60; the default is 2.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Exit after that many screens.
.TP
.BI \-s " order"
Order the processes by
.B cpu
(processor share, the default),
.B mem
(size) or
.B pid.
.TP
.B \-n
Write no color.
.TP
.B \-p
Plain output: each screen as ASCII lines with no escape sequence, the
default when the output is not a terminal.
.PP
While it runs on a terminal
.I picotop
reads these keys:
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B "c m p"
Order the processes by processor share, size or process id.
.TP
.B "+ \-"
Lengthen or shorten the interval by one second.
.TP
.B space
Refresh at once.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
picofetch(1), ps(1), vmstat(1), sysctl(8)
